    "debrief.title": "From repeated SSH failures to privileged activity",
    "debrief.summary": "Repeated failures from one source are followed by password authentication for deploy and sudo activity. Link the failure counts, explicit success, and use of privileges in that order.",
    "debrief.stages.0.title": "Separate routine activity from concentrated failures",
    "debrief.stages.0.detail": "198.51.100.73 has 60 Failed password records targeting admin, root, and deploy. Handle legitimate public-key logins and CRON sessions separately, and account for field shifts caused by invalid user.",
    "debrief.stages.1.title": "Connect failure and success using the same identifiers",
    "debrief.stages.1.detail": "The last failure is at 09:17:52 UTC. Fifty seconds later, at 09:18:42, the same source has Accepted password for deploy. Use this explicit success and the following session start, rather than the impression created by many failures.",
    "debrief.stages.2.title": "Check privileges used after authentication",
    "debrief.stages.2.detail": "deploy has two sudo commands: id and the command requesting an nginx restart. Correlate COMMAND with USER=root; do not count sudo session opening and closing as extra commands.",
    "debrief.stages.3.title": "Distinguish execution from service outcome",
    "debrief.stages.3.detail": "The privileged restart command is observable, but no service log confirms that nginx restarted successfully and continued running. Limit the finding to successful authentication and subsequent privileged commands.",
    "debrief.cause": "The logs show password authentication being accepted and deploy using sudo. They cannot establish whether a password was guessed, leaked credentials were used, or a legitimate user performed the actions.",
    "debrief.impact": "Successful authentication for deploy and two sudo commands targeting root are confirmed in this dataset. Persistence, access to other files, movement to other hosts, and service outages are not observed.",
    "debrief.limits.0": "A source IP does not identify a real person.",
    "debrief.limits.1": "admin marked invalid user does not establish that the account exists.",
    "debrief.limits.2": "These are static synthetic logs. No real SSH connection or restart occurs.",
    "debrief.remediation.0": "Review business requirements, reduce password-based SSH, and combine keys or additional authentication with source restrictions.",
    "debrief.remediation.1": "Correlate increased failures for a source or account with immediate successes and sudo activity.",
    "debrief.remediation.2": "Restrict sudo for service accounts such as deploy to necessary operations and review broad privileges.",
    "debrief.remediation.3": "When suspicious authentication is confirmed, preserve evidence, investigate the credentials and existing sessions, and revoke them as appropriate.",
    "debrief.lessons.0": "Count each authentication result separately from session lines.",
    "debrief.lessons.1": "Matching sources, accounts, and timestamps support event correlation.",
    "debrief.lessons.2": "Verify the requested command and the service outcome separately.",
    "debrief.knowledge.0.title": "SSH methods and authentication results",
    "debrief.knowledge.0.detail": "SSH distinguishes methods such as password and public-key authentication. A success records that access to the account was allowed using that method. Do not identify a person from an IP or assume every success is malicious; compare the usual method, client, and time of day.",
    "debrief.knowledge.1.title": "Read authentication, sessions, and sudo as separate events",
    "debrief.knowledge.1.detail": "Authentication results, session opening and closing, and sudo COMMAND entries have different units. In these sudo records, distinguish the invoking user from the execution identity in USER. A recorded command does not establish completion of the restart or subsequent healthy operation.",
    "debrief.knowledge.2.title": "Check log structure and the population before counting",
    "debrief.knowledge.2.detail": "The invalid user phrase changes username and IP field positions here. Extract failed authentications first, then count target names and sources. Attempts against several names from one IP suggest probing, but counts alone do not prove a leaked password or successful intrusion.",
    "debrief.history.0.title": "Analysis of SSH authentication attempts by a Mirai variant",
    "debrief.history.0.detail": "Unit 42 analyzed Mirai-related activity observed in 2021 and reported an SSH brute-force capability using hard-coded credentials and other inputs. It illustrates attempts against weak credentials used alongside other entry paths.",
    "debrief.history.0.relation": "Compare the mechanism of SSH authentication attempts. This exercise does not recreate Mirai infection or a botnet, nor imply the same attacker, victim, or credentials.",
    "debrief.references.0.description": "Primary specification defining authentication methods such as public key and password and the success or failure of authentication requests.",
    "debrief.references.1.description": "Explains settings such as AuthenticationMethods, PasswordAuthentication, and PubkeyAuthentication. It does not describe the actual configuration of the fictional server.",
    "debrief.references.2.title": "Unit 42 — Mirai variant and SSH authentication attempts (2021)",
    "debrief.references.2.description": "A report by the researchers who analyzed the activity. Read the SSH sections while comparing similarities and differences with the exercise.",
    "fileTexts.normalReadme": "SSH authentication anomalies and sudo activity — Normal logs before the incident\n\nAll organizations, people, accounts, communication, and logs in this exercise are fictional.\nIP addresses use documentation ranges; domains use .test / .example.\nDate: August 12, 2026. All log timestamps are UTC (Japan time is UTC+9).\n\nThese are the normal logs before the incident, including routine business and administration. Press the attack button to switch to logs containing prepared static exercise events. No communication or commands are transmitted or executed. Returning to normal restores this state.\n\nInvestigation targets:\n  /var/log/auth.log\n\nBasic operations: pwd, ls, cd, cat, head, tail, less, grep, awk, cut, sort, uniq, wc, sed\nExample (original placeholder names): grep -n \"pattern\" /var/log/example.log\nCombine filters and counts with pipes. For help, enter help.\nThis terminal simulates log analysis; it uses no real OS or external communication.\nSeparate observations from hypotheses and verify agreement across logs.\n\nCompare normal and incident logs to find added lines and different processing results. Legitimate input can also contain punctuation such as apostrophes.\n",
    "fileTexts.attackedReadme": "Case 1: SSH authentication anomalies and sudo activity\n\nAll organizations, people, accounts, communication, and logs in this exercise are fictional.\nIP addresses use documentation ranges; domains use .test / .example.\nDate: August 12, 2026. All log timestamps are UTC (Japan time is UTC+9).\n\nMonitoring reported a rapid increase in failed SSH authentication. Investigate successful authentication records and the privileged commands that followed.\n\nInvestigation targets:\n  /var/log/auth.log\n\nBasic operations: pwd, ls, cd, cat, head, tail, less, grep, awk, cut, sort, uniq, wc, sed\nExample (original placeholder names): grep -n \"pattern\" /var/log/example.log\nCombine filters and counts with pipes. For help, enter help.\nThis terminal simulates log analysis; it uses no real OS or external communication.\nSeparate observations from hypotheses and verify agreement across logs.\n",
    "context.hosts.0.address": "Exercise server (its own IP is not supplied and is outside the investigation scope)"
