    "debrief.remediation.1": "Minimize export permissions, datasets, and row counts. Consider reauthentication or approval for sensitive exports.",
    "debrief.remediation.2": "Review exposure of admin, configuration, and backup paths, and enable investigation across probing requests and later authentication.",
    "debrief.remediation.3": "Keep shared request IDs in HTTP and business audit records, preserving both denials and successes.",
    "debrief.lessons.0": "HTTP 200 does not equal business-operation success.",
    "debrief.lessons.1": "Request IDs connect access records to actual processing results.",
    "debrief.lessons.2": "Expect successes and denials to coexist in one timeline.",
    "debrief.knowledge.0.title": "HTTP access logs versus business outcomes",
    "debrief.knowledge.0.detail": "Nginx combined format records a request, status, response-body byte count, and other fields. Body size is not CSV row count, and HTTP 200 does not guarantee every business operation succeeded. Use the added request_id to match the application action, result, and count.",
    "debrief.knowledge.1.title": "Distinguish authentication from authorization",
    "debrief.knowledge.1.detail": "Authentication verifies an account’s identity; authorization decides whether that identity may perform a particular action. Logging in does not grant access to all data. Check success and denial for each export resource using matching accounts and sources.",
    "debrief.knowledge.2.title": "A request ID supports correlation; it is not evidence by itself",
    "debrief.knowledge.2.detail": "An application request ID links multiple events from one operation. Also check timestamps, targets, sources, and accounts for consistency. Access and audit records are different observations of the same request, so adding both line counts would overcount requests.",
    "debrief.history.0.title": "Confluence broken access control: CVE-2023-22515",
    "debrief.history.0.detail": "In October 2023, Atlassian disclosed broken access control in Confluence Data Center and Server. Unauthorized administrator-account creation and access were reported, and guidance recommended checking for unexpected accounts and access records.",
    "debrief.history.0.relation": "This related example helps explain why authentication state or HTTP outcomes alone cannot establish that permissions are appropriate. The lab does not recreate Confluence or this CVE, and its CSV-export attack path differs.",
    "debrief.references.0.description": "Documents log_format and standard combined format, providing a basis for reading body bytes, requests, and status fields.",
    "debrief.references.1.description": "Explains logging when, where, who, and what, and correlating events with an Interaction identifier.",
    "debrief.references.2.description": "Covers authentication versus authorization, least privilege, and permission checks on every request.",
    "debrief.references.3.title": "Atlassian — Official CVE-2023-22515 advisory",
    "debrief.references.3.description": "The vendor’s official material on the access-control flaw disclosed in 2023. Keep the exercise conditions separate from those of the real product.",
    "fileTexts.normalReadme": "Correlating Web probing and data exports — Normal logs before the incident\n\nAll organizations, people, accounts, communication, and logs in this exercise are fictional.\nIP addresses use documentation ranges; domains use .test / .example.\nDate: August 12, 2026. All log timestamps are UTC (Japan time is UTC+9).\n\nThese are the normal logs before the incident, including routine business and administration. Press the attack button to switch to logs containing prepared static exercise events. No communication or commands are transmitted or executed. Returning to normal restores this state.\n\nInvestigation targets:\n  /var/log/nginx/access.log\n  /var/log/app/audit.log\n\nBasic operations: pwd, ls, cd, cat, head, tail, less, grep, awk, cut, sort, uniq, wc, sed\nExample (original placeholder names): grep -n \"pattern\" /var/log/example.log\nCombine filters and counts with pipes. For help, enter help.\nThis terminal simulates log analysis; it uses no real OS or external communication.\nSeparate observations from hypotheses and verify agreement across logs.\n\nCompare normal and incident logs to find added lines and different processing results. Legitimate input can also contain punctuation such as apostrophes.\n",
    "fileTexts.attackedReadme": "Case 2: Correlating Web probing and data exports\n\nAll organizations, people, accounts, communication, and logs in this exercise are fictional.\nIP addresses use documentation ranges; domains use .test / .example.\nDate: August 12, 2026. All log timestamps are UTC (Japan time is UTC+9).\n\nProbing of administrative pages was observed. Correlate Web access and application audit logs to identify operations that actually succeeded.\n\nInvestigation targets:\n  /var/log/nginx/access.log\n  /var/log/app/audit.log\n\nBasic operations: pwd, ls, cd, cat, head, tail, less, grep, awk, cut, sort, uniq, wc, sed\nExample (original placeholder names): grep -n \"pattern\" /var/log/example.log\nCombine filters and counts with pipes. For help, enter help.\nThis terminal simulates log analysis; it uses no real OS or external communication.\nSeparate observations from hypotheses and verify agreement across logs.\n",
