    "debrief.limits.2": "No message bodies, attachments, endpoint execution histories, or business authorization records are provided.",
    "debrief.remediation.0": "Correlate endpoint and business-owner evidence to check whether these transmissions were authorized.",
    "debrief.remediation.1": "Limit outbound traffic and SMTP submission to required destinations and identities; monitor unknown destinations and unusual volumes.",
    "debrief.remediation.2": "Restrict where service SMTP credentials can be used, and review unnecessary credentials or overly broad usage.",
    "debrief.remediation.3": "Synchronize DNS, proxy, and mail timestamps and retain client identifiers and queue IDs for correlation.",
    "debrief.lessons.0": "Correlated activity and confirmed data exfiltration are different conclusions.",
    "debrief.lessons.1": "Distinguish hostnames, MX, SMTP authentication user, From, and recipient.",
    "debrief.lessons.2": "Byte totals require a defined channel, direction, and set of filters.",
    "debrief.knowledge.0.title": "Name resolution and connection are separate observations",
    "debrief.knowledge.0.detail": "DNS looks up addresses and other resource information from names. Receiving an A answer does not mean later connection or transmission succeeds. Hostnames can serve different roles, such as mail relay; match the full queried name with actual destinations in proxy and delivery logs.",
    "debrief.knowledge.1.title": "SMTP acceptance does not mean the mail was read",
    "debrief.knowledge.1.detail": "When an SMTP receiver returns success after message data, it accepts responsibility for delivery or relay. status=sent and 250 support that acceptance here, not human reading. Follow acceptance, recipient, and delivery with the queue ID, and distinguish the authentication user from the From address.",
    "debrief.knowledge.2.title": "Align direction, units, and scope before totaling traffic",
    "debrief.knowledge.2.detail": "Here bytes_out is outward from the client and bytes_in belongs to the response. Match client, host, method, and path before totaling; exclude full-message mail size and unrelated GETs. Higher volume is an investigative lead, but content-free logs cannot establish sensitive-data loss or business legitimacy.",
    "debrief.history.0.title": "DNS and HTTP analysis of SolarWinds SUNBURST",
    "debrief.history.0.detail": "In December 2020, FireEye disclosed SUNBURST embedded in SolarWinds Orion updates. Its analysis related DNS responses to subsequent HTTP communication. Traffic resembling normal product activity also needs contextual investigation.",
    "debrief.history.0.relation": "This is a reference for analysis across multiple traffic logs. The exercise recreates neither SUNBURST C2 nor supply-chain compromise; its mail and upload events do not reproduce the actual incident.",
